PathDB RPQ corpus
------------------

The `pathdb` workload (ADR-0479, LABS-646) derives its 26 abstract regular-path patterns / 142 concrete queries from
dbgutalca/pathdb-eval1 (https://github.com/dbgutalca/pathdb-eval1) at commit
e62e33c2a40aee3cecca35961dd6da94a575c4f1 (2026-03-31), extracted from its `Queries.xlsx`:

  - workloads/pathdb/queries/p001.cypher ... p142.cypher: OUR Cypher quantified-path translations of the 142
    queries (the label map and translation rules are recorded in workloads/pathdb/README.md), each file's header
    naming the upstream repo and pin.
  - workloads/pathdb/restrictors/r001.cypher ... r065.cypher: our path-mode re-spellings of the 13 templates
    across five path modes.

Not taken: upstream's Google Drive data link, jars or evaluation machinery — the corpus runs over the same pinned
LDBC SNB data set as the `graph-benchmark-ldbc` workload above, and its seed substitutions (the degree-median policy of
workloads/pathdb/seed-keys.json and params-sf<SF>.json) are ours.

The translated queries are used under upstream's Apache-2.0 licence (the full text is the repository root
LICENSE); the translations themselves are this repository's work under Apache-2.0.

Fraud QPP suite
---------------

The `fraud-qpp` workload (ADR-0479) is IN-HOUSE: its queries, its sample fixture and its oracle are this
repository's work. The unlicensed external repos and the ©-Neo4j training material that inspired the suite were
never copied (verified 2026-10-31); the only facts carried over from the external spike run are performance and
oracle reference points, recorded in workloads/fraud-qpp/oracle.json's `_source`.
